-- Q1: medium / L2 — Botnet command-and-control infrastructure
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Greenfield Consulting's network security team discovers that 15 workstations are periodically sending encrypted heartbeat signals to an external IP address every 60 seconds. The workstations continue functioning normally. Investigation reveals the machines are awaiting instructions from a remote server controlled by an attacker. This network of compromised machines is BEST described as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A botnet controlled through a command-and-control server", "A peer-to-peer file sharing network misconfigured by employees", "A distributed computing cluster created by authorized IT staff", "A virtual private network tunnel established by remote workers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-83 Rev. 1 defines a botnet as a network of compromised computers (bots) remotely controlled by an attacker through command-and-control (C2) infrastructure. The periodic heartbeat signals and awaiting instructions are hallmarks of C2 communication. Wrong (B): Peer-to-peer traffic would show bidirectional file transfers, not periodic heartbeat signals to a single external IP. Wrong (C): Authorized distributed computing would be documented and managed through legitimate infrastructure. Wrong (D): VPN tunnels are established intentionally by users and would appear in network configuration, not as covert periodic signals.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — CVSS base score components
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Brookfield Financial's security analyst is evaluating a newly disclosed vulnerability using the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). The vulnerability requires network access, no privileges, and no user interaction to exploit. It affects confidentiality and integrity but not availability. The analyst must calculate the base score. Which set of metrics comprises the CVSS base score?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exploitability metrics only, measuring how easy the vulnerability is to exploit", "Attack vector, attack complexity, privileges required, user interaction, scope, and CIA impact", "Base metrics plus temporal metrics reflecting current exploit availability", "Environmental metrics adjusted for the organization's specific infrastructure"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST NVD and CVSS v3.1 specification define the base score as comprising exploitability metrics (attack vector, attack complexity, privileges required, user interaction, scope) and impact metrics (confidentiality, integrity, availability). These represent the intrinsic qualities of the vulnerability. Wrong (A): Exploitability metrics are only one component of the base score; impact metrics are equally required. Wrong (C): Temporal metrics (exploit maturity, remediation level, report confidence) are a separate optional group that modifies the base score. Wrong (D): Environmental metrics represent organization-specific adjustments and are calculated separately from the base score.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Lateral movement in post-compromise
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Harmon & Associates' SOC team detects that an attacker who initially compromised a marketing workstation is now using stolen service account credentials to access file shares on the finance server and query the HR database. The attacker is methodically moving through the internal network to locate high-value data. This post-compromise activity is BEST categorized as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Initial access through credential harvesting", "Reconnaissance of external network services", "Lateral movement across the internal network", "Data exfiltration to an external staging server"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): MITRE ATT&CK Tactic TA0008 defines lateral movement as techniques attackers use to move through an environment after initial access, pivoting from one system to another using stolen credentials or exploits. The scenario describes classic lateral movement from marketing to finance to HR systems. Wrong (A): Initial access (TA0001) refers to gaining the first foothold, which already occurred when the marketing workstation was compromised. Wrong (B): Reconnaissance of external services occurs before initial access; the attacker is already inside the network. Wrong (D): Data exfiltration (TA0010) involves transferring data outside the network, but the attacker is still locating data internally.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Business logic flaw exploitation
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Clarkson Tax Services deploys a client portal where users submit estimated tax payment amounts. A penetration tester discovers that the payment processing API accepts negative payment values, which the application interprets as refunds credited to the user's linked bank account. Traditional input validation checks for data type and length pass successfully because the negative number is a valid integer within the allowed range. Standard vulnerability scanners did not flag this issue. This finding BEST illustrates:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A SQL injection vulnerability in the payment processing module", "A cross-site request forgery weakness in the API endpoint", "A buffer overflow caused by unexpected numeric input", "A business logic flaw that technical controls alone cannot detect"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): OWASP identifies business logic flaws as vulnerabilities arising from legitimate application functionality being used in unintended ways. Accepting negative payments is syntactically valid input but violates business rules. These flaws require manual review or logic-aware testing because automated scanners check for technical vulnerabilities, not business rule violations. Wrong (A): SQL injection involves inserting database commands into input fields; a negative number processed normally is not injection. Wrong (B): CSRF involves forging requests from an authenticated user's browser to another site, not manipulating payment values. Wrong (C): Buffer overflow involves exceeding memory allocation; a negative integer within the valid range does not overflow a buffer.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — DNS tunneling for covert exfiltration
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Westbrook Advisory's security team notices that a compromised workstation is generating an unusually high volume of DNS queries to a single external domain. The queries contain long, encoded subdomains (e.g., aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ.attacker-domain.com), and the DNS responses contain TXT records with similarly encoded payloads. Firewall logs show no blocked outbound connections because DNS traffic on port 53 is permitted. The attacker is MOST likely using this technique to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exfiltrate data through DNS tunneling, bypassing firewall restrictions", "Perform a DNS amplification DDoS attack against the external domain", "Poison the organization's DNS cache to redirect internal traffic", "Establish a rogue DNS server to intercept employee web browsing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-81-2 identifies DNS tunneling as a technique that encodes data within DNS queries and responses to create a covert communication channel. The encoded subdomains carry exfiltrated data outbound, while TXT response records carry commands inbound, all over permitted port 53 traffic that firewalls typically allow. Wrong (B): DNS amplification attacks use open resolvers to flood a victim with amplified responses; this scenario shows encoded queries to one domain, not amplification. Wrong (C): DNS cache poisoning modifies cached DNS records to redirect traffic; the scenario describes outbound encoded queries, not cache manipulation. Wrong (D): A rogue DNS server intercepts resolution requests; this attacker is sending queries to an external domain, not serving DNS responses internally.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Polymorphic malware evading signatures
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Redmond Financial's endpoint protection team discovers that a malware sample on a workstation has rewritten its own code each time it executes, changing its binary signature while maintaining the same malicious functionality. The firm's signature-based antivirus repeatedly fails to detect subsequent instances. The security team must recommend a detection approach. Which control would MOST effectively detect this type of malware?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Updating antivirus signature databases more frequently", "Implementing behavior-based endpoint detection and response (EDR)", "Increasing the frequency of full system vulnerability scans", "Deploying a network intrusion detection system with signature rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-83 Rev. 1 recommends behavior-based detection for polymorphic malware that changes its signature on each execution. EDR solutions monitor process behavior, memory patterns, and system call sequences rather than relying on static signatures, making them effective against self-modifying code. Wrong (A): More frequent signature updates cannot keep pace with malware that generates a new signature on every execution cycle. Wrong (C): Vulnerability scans identify missing patches and misconfigurations, not active malware infections on endpoints. Wrong (D): Network IDS with signature rules faces the same limitation as host-based signature detection when the malware's network traffic patterns also change.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Adware definition
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$What type of unwanted software automatically displays or downloads advertising material, often bundled with free applications?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ransomware", "Spyware", "Adware", "Scareware"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-83 classifies adware as software that automatically renders advertisements, often bundled with legitimate free programs. While less destructive than other malware, adware can degrade performance and may track browsing habits. Wrong (A): Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment for the decryption key, not display advertisements. Wrong (B): Spyware secretly monitors user activity and collects sensitive data such as keystrokes, not primarily advertising content. Wrong (D): Scareware displays fake security warnings to trick users into purchasing unnecessary software, not general advertisements.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Reconnaissance phase of attack lifecycle
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Langston & Partners' threat intelligence team discovers that an unknown actor has been querying the firm's public DNS records, scanning its external IP ranges for open ports, and harvesting employee names and titles from LinkedIn. No exploitation attempts have been detected. This pre-attack activity is classified in the cyber kill chain as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exploitation of a known vulnerability", "Command and control establishment", "Delivery of a malicious payload", "Reconnaissance to gather target information"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain identifies reconnaissance as the first phase where attackers gather information about the target including network topology, open services, and personnel details. DNS queries, port scanning, and social media harvesting without exploitation attempts are hallmarks of this phase. Wrong (A): Exploitation occurs after a payload has been delivered and involves actively compromising a vulnerability, which has not occurred here. Wrong (B): Command and control is established after successful exploitation to maintain communication with compromised systems. Wrong (C): Delivery involves transmitting the attack payload to the target, such as sending a phishing email, which has not yet occurred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — MITRE ATT&CK framework purpose
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Evergreen Financial's CISO proposes adopting the MITRE ATT&CK framework to improve the firm's threat detection capabilities. The security team will map existing detection rules to the framework's matrix. The PRIMARY purpose of the MITRE ATT&CK framework is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Provide a comprehensive knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques based on real-world observations", "Calculate numeric risk scores for individual vulnerabilities using standardized metrics", "Certify that an organization's security controls meet regulatory compliance requirements", "Define minimum encryption standards for protecting data at rest and in transit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): MITRE ATT&CK is a globally accessible knowledge base of adversary tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) based on real-world observations. Organizations use it to identify detection gaps by mapping their security controls against known attack techniques across the kill chain. Wrong (B): CVSS, not MITRE ATT&CK, provides numeric scoring for vulnerabilities. ATT&CK catalogs attack behaviors rather than scoring individual vulnerabilities. Wrong (C): MITRE ATT&CK is a threat intelligence framework, not a compliance certification program like SOC 2 or ISO 27001. Wrong (D): Encryption standards are defined by NIST SP 800-175B and related publications, not by the MITRE ATT&CK framework.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Replay attack definition
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$What type of attack involves intercepting a valid network transmission and retransmitting it later to fraudulently gain access or repeat a transaction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Brute force attack", "Replay attack", "Birthday attack", "Side-channel attack"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-63B defines a replay attack as one where an attacker captures a valid authentication token or data transmission and retransmits it to impersonate the original sender or duplicate a transaction. Timestamps and nonces are common defenses. Wrong (A): A brute force attack systematically tries all possible combinations to guess a password or key, not retransmit captured data. Wrong (C): A birthday attack exploits hash collision probability to find two inputs that produce the same hash output. Wrong (D): A side-channel attack extracts information from physical characteristics of a system such as power consumption or electromagnetic emissions.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Honeypot deception technology
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Mercer CPA Group deploys a server on its internal network configured to mimic a database containing fabricated client financial records. The server has no legitimate business purpose and is monitored around the clock. When an attacker accesses this system, the security team receives an immediate alert with detailed forensic data about the attacker's methods. This deception-based security control is known as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A demilitarized zone (DMZ) server", "An intrusion prevention system (IPS)", "A honeypot designed to detect and study attackers", "A disaster recovery failover system"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-83 describes a honeypot as a decoy system designed to attract attackers, detect unauthorized access attempts, and gather intelligence about attack methods. Since the server has no legitimate purpose, any access is inherently suspicious. Wrong (A): A DMZ server sits between external and internal networks to host public-facing services; it serves legitimate traffic, unlike a decoy. Wrong (B): An IPS actively blocks malicious traffic inline but does not impersonate a target system to attract attackers. Wrong (D): A disaster recovery system provides business continuity during outages, not deception capabilities for threat detection.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L2 — Shoulder surfing scenario
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$An employee at Ashford & Co. is logging into the firm's client portal at an airport lounge. A person seated nearby watches the employee type credentials on the laptop screen. The observer later uses those credentials to access the portal. This social engineering technique is classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Phishing through email impersonation", "Dumpster diving for discarded documents", "Pretexting to manipulate the employee", "Shoulder surfing to observe sensitive information"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-12 Rev. 1 identifies shoulder surfing as a social engineering technique where an attacker directly observes a victim entering sensitive information such as passwords, PINs, or account numbers in a public or shared space. Wrong (A): Phishing uses fraudulent electronic communications to trick victims, not direct visual observation of credentials. Wrong (B): Dumpster diving involves searching through discarded materials for sensitive information, not live observation. Wrong (C): Pretexting involves creating a fabricated scenario to manipulate someone into divulging information; no interaction with the victim occurred here.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Race condition vulnerability exploitation
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Millbrook Financial's web application allows clients to transfer funds between accounts. A penetration tester discovers that by sending two simultaneous transfer requests of $10,000 from an account with only $10,000 balance, both requests pass the balance validation check before either deducts the funds, resulting in $20,000 transferred from a $10,000 balance. The application uses no transaction locking. The development team proposes four fixes: (1) adding client-side JavaScript validation, (2) implementing database-level row locking on the balance check, (3) adding a CAPTCHA to the transfer form, and (4) increasing the server's processing speed. Which fix addresses the ROOT CAUSE?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Implementing database-level row locking to serialize concurrent balance checks", "Adding client-side JavaScript validation to prevent duplicate submissions", "Adding a CAPTCHA to prevent automated concurrent requests", "Increasing server processing speed to complete transactions faster"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): OWASP identifies race conditions (also called time-of-check-to-time-of-use or TOCTOU vulnerabilities) as flaws where concurrent operations access shared resources without proper synchronization. Database-level row locking serializes access to the balance record, ensuring one transaction completes before the next reads the balance. Wrong (B): Client-side validation is easily bypassed by an attacker using direct HTTP requests or automated tools, providing no server-side protection against concurrent requests. Wrong (C): CAPTCHA slows automated submissions but does not prevent two simultaneous legitimate-appearing requests from creating a race condition. Wrong (D): Faster processing reduces the race window but does not eliminate it; without locking, sufficiently fast concurrent requests can still exploit the gap.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Cryptographic failures (OWASP #2)
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$During a security audit of Pemberton Accounting's client portal, auditors discover that client Social Security numbers are stored in the database using the MD5 hashing algorithm without salting. An attacker who gains database access could use precomputed rainbow tables to reverse the hashes rapidly. Under the OWASP Top 10, this weakness is classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Broken access control allowing unauthorized data retrieval", "Cryptographic failures exposing sensitive data through weak hashing", "Security misconfiguration in the database server settings", "Injection vulnerability in the data storage layer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): OWASP Top 10 A02:2021 (Cryptographic Failures) covers weaknesses where sensitive data is exposed due to weak or missing cryptographic protections. MD5 is a deprecated hash algorithm vulnerable to rainbow table attacks, and the absence of salting further weakens the protection. Wrong (A): Broken access control (A01) addresses permission failures, not the weakness of the cryptographic method used to protect stored data. Wrong (C): Security misconfiguration (A05) covers default settings and unnecessary features, not the choice of cryptographic algorithm for data protection. Wrong (D): Injection (A03) involves untrusted data sent to an interpreter as commands, not the method used to hash stored data.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Dumpster diving definition
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$What social engineering technique involves searching through an organization's discarded materials to find sensitive information such as passwords, network diagrams, or financial records?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Tailgating", "Baiting", "Dumpster diving", "Pretexting"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-12 Rev. 1 identifies dumpster diving as a social engineering method where attackers search through trash and recycling bins for improperly disposed documents containing sensitive information such as credentials, organizational charts, or financial data. Wrong (A): Tailgating involves following an authorized person through a secured entrance without proper authentication. Wrong (B): Baiting involves leaving a malware-infected device in a location where a target will find and use it. Wrong (D): Pretexting involves creating a fabricated identity or scenario to manipulate a target into providing information or access.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Scareware definition
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$What type of malicious software displays fake security warnings or virus alerts to frighten users into purchasing unnecessary or harmful software?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Adware", "Ransomware", "A worm", "Scareware"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-83 identifies scareware as malicious software that uses social engineering to generate anxiety, displaying fabricated security alerts or infection warnings to pressure users into paying for fake security tools or downloading additional malware. Wrong (A): Adware displays advertising content for revenue, not fake security warnings designed to frighten users into purchasing software. Wrong (B): Ransomware encrypts actual files and demands payment for decryption keys, not fake alerts about nonexistent threats. Wrong (C): A worm is self-replicating malware that spreads across networks without user interaction, not software that displays fake warnings.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: hard / L3 — Steganography for covert data exfiltration
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Crestview CPA Group's data loss prevention (DLP) system monitors email attachments and web uploads for sensitive patterns such as Social Security numbers and account numbers. A forensic investigation reveals that an insider exfiltrated 2,000 client records by embedding the data within the pixel values of image files posted to an external collaboration platform. The DLP system did not flag the uploads because the files appeared to be ordinary JPEG photographs. This covert data hiding technique is BEST described as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Steganography, which conceals data within ordinary-looking media files", "Encryption, which scrambles data to prevent unauthorized reading", "Data masking, which replaces sensitive values with fictitious data", "Tokenization, which substitutes sensitive data with non-sensitive placeholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-101 Rev. 1 defines steganography as the practice of concealing information within other non-secret data or physical objects, such as embedding text within image pixel values. Unlike encryption, steganography hides the existence of the message itself, making it particularly effective at evading content-inspection DLP systems. Wrong (B): Encryption makes data unreadable without a key but does not hide its existence; encrypted files are detectable as ciphertext by DLP systems. Wrong (C): Data masking replaces real values with realistic fictional values for non-production use and is a privacy technique, not a data exfiltration method. Wrong (D): Tokenization replaces sensitive data with non-sensitive tokens maintained in a secure vault and is a data protection control, not an exfiltration technique.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Vulnerable and outdated components (OWASP #6)
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Thornberry Accounting's development team uses an open-source JavaScript library for PDF rendering in its client portal. A security scan reveals the library version is three years old and has four published CVEs, two rated critical. The team has not tracked the library version since initial deployment. Under the OWASP Top 10, this risk is categorized as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Injection through the JavaScript rendering engine", "Vulnerable and outdated components with known security flaws", "Insecure design in the application architecture", "Server-side request forgery through PDF processing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): OWASP Top 10 A06:2021 (Vulnerable and Outdated Components) addresses risks from using libraries, frameworks, or software with known vulnerabilities. Organizations that do not track component versions or monitor for published CVEs are exposed to exploitation of known flaws. Wrong (A): Injection (A03) involves sending malicious input to an interpreter; using an outdated library is a supply chain and component management issue. Wrong (C): Insecure design (A04) refers to fundamental architectural flaws in security control design, not the failure to update third-party dependencies. Wrong (D): SSRF (A10) involves server-side requests to attacker-controlled URLs; the issue here is outdated software components, not request forgery.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Threat intelligence sharing and ISACs
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Franklin & Webb CPAs joins the Financial Services Information Sharing and Analysis Center (FS-ISAC). Within the first month, the firm receives an alert about a new phishing campaign targeting accounting firms with malicious Excel attachments. The firm's email gateway team uses the provided file hashes to create blocking rules before any employees receive the phishing emails. This scenario BEST demonstrates the value of:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Regulatory compliance auditing through third-party assessors", "Annual penetration testing performed by external consultants", "Sector-specific threat intelligence sharing enabling proactive defense", "Internal vulnerability scanning on a quarterly schedule"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-150 (Guide to Cyber Threat Information Sharing) emphasizes that participating in ISACs enables organizations to receive timely, sector-specific threat intelligence including indicators of compromise. Using shared file hashes to block threats before they arrive demonstrates proactive defense through intelligence sharing. Wrong (A): Compliance auditing evaluates adherence to standards after the fact, not real-time threat intelligence distribution. Wrong (B): Penetration testing identifies vulnerabilities in existing systems but does not provide early warning of new external campaigns. Wrong (D): Quarterly vulnerability scanning addresses internal weaknesses on a fixed schedule and cannot provide real-time intelligence about emerging external threats.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Open redirect vulnerability exploitation
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$Dalton Advisory Group's client portal includes a login page with a redirect parameter: /login?redirect=/dashboard. A security consultant discovers that an attacker can modify this to /login?redirect=https://evil-site.com. After a client logs in normally, the application redirects them to the attacker's site, which displays a fake session timeout message and harvests their credentials a second time. This vulnerability is classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Cross-site scripting injecting malicious code into the login page", "SQL injection manipulating the redirect query parameter", "Broken authentication allowing session token theft", "An open redirect enabling phishing through a trusted domain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): OWASP identifies unvalidated redirects and forwards as vulnerabilities where applications redirect users to attacker-controlled URLs. The trusted domain in the original URL lends credibility to the phishing attempt because users see the legitimate site before being redirected. Wrong (A): XSS involves injecting executable scripts into web pages; modifying a URL redirect parameter to an external site is not script injection. Wrong (B): SQL injection sends database commands through input fields; the redirect parameter manipulates application routing, not database queries. Wrong (C): Broken authentication involves flaws in session management or credential verification; the authentication itself works correctly here, but the post-login redirect is exploited.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Typosquatting attack
(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities', $EXPL$An employee at Birchwood CPA intends to visit the IRS website at irs.gov but accidentally types irs-gov.com. The misspelled domain hosts a convincing replica of the real IRS site and prompts the employee to enter login credentials. This attack technique that relies on common typing mistakes is known as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Typosquatting, which registers domains similar to legitimate sites", "Phishing through fraudulent email communication", "DNS cache poisoning that redirects legitimate domain queries", "Cross-site scripting that modifies the displayed URL"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST defines typosquatting (also called URL hijacking) as the practice of registering domain names that are common misspellings or variations of legitimate websites. Attackers rely on users making typographical errors to direct them to malicious sites that impersonate the intended destination. Wrong (B): Phishing uses fraudulent emails or messages to lure victims; typosquatting relies on user typing errors when entering URLs directly. Wrong (C): DNS cache poisoning modifies DNS resolver records to redirect queries for legitimate domains; here the user typed a different domain entirely. Wrong (D): XSS injects malicious scripts into web pages but does not involve registering lookalike domain names.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2)
;

COMMIT;
